[Line "Your move: 5.f3 O-O 6.Qd2 e5 (Zaichik's gambit)"]
[Section "Your moves against the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "Castling at once against the 150 Attack with f3, then striking at d4 before the Bh6 and h4-h5 attack is ready. Only the first two moves are sourced; the continuation is a gap in the notes."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 Nf6 5. f3 ( 5. Qd2 O-O { The same position arises after 6.f3. White has other sixth moves here (6.Nf3, 6.Bh6) that no source in these
notes covers. } 6. f3 )
5... O-O { [%cal Re3h6,Rd2h6,Rh2h4]
The second choice next to 5...c6, and the most played move here among 2400+ players. It is the move that the study
literature warns about most: with the king on g8, Qd2, Bh6 and h4-h5 have a target, and the Spanish study on the 150 Attack
(313 likes) shows the pattern with 6.Qd2 c6 7.Bh6 b5 8.h4 Nbd7 9.h5 Nxh5? 10.Rxh5 gxh5 11.Qg5 as what happens to a player who
castles and then ignores it. The same study adds that analysis found no great risk if White plays the attack too hastily.
Wikipedia dates the idea of castling anyway to Gennady Zaichik, about 1985, who found a dangerous gambit with 6...e5 against
Qd2. **Disagreement between sources:** the 5.Qd2 chapter of a White-side study (46 likes) marks the same castling as a
mistake, because it lets White develop and castle long with tempo. Treat this as a second choice, not as the main line.
}
6. Qd2 e5 { [%cal Ge5d4,Re3h6] Zaichik's move: the centre is challenged before White has castled and before Bh6 can be played
with effect. The sources name the move and call it a gambit but give no moves after it, so nothing beyond this point is
claimed here. For orientation only: in 2400+ games White answers most often with 7.Nge2 (a Sämisch-like set-up, but with
the pawn still on c2), then 7.d5, 7.O-O-O and 7.dxe5. **Gap:** a book or a well-liked study on 4.Be3 Nf6 5.f3 O-O 6.Qd2 e5 is
needed before this line can be drilled any further. } *

[Line "Your move: 5.Qd2 Ng4?! is a loss of time: 5...c6"]
[Section "Your moves against the 150 Attack"]
[Summary "Hitting the bishop with ...Ng4 before White has played f3. The sources call it a waste of time: the bishop steps aside and h3 drives the knight back, so the notes keep 5...c6."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 Nf6 5. Qd2 { Without f3 the knight on f6 can still go to g4. That is deliberate: the
British players who made this system popular concluded that the Argentine fear of ...Ng4 was exaggerated, and they left
f3 out. } 5... c6 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gd8a5] The notes' move: the same Czech-Pirc set-up as after 5.f3 c6, with the knight kept on f6
where it eyes e4 and the c-pawn already played. Nothing is lost on the move order, because 6.f3 b5 is the same position as in the
previous chapters. }
( 5... Ng4?! { [%cal Rg4e3] Attacks the bishop and looks active, but White simply moves it, and Black has spent a move to put the knight on
a square from which it will be driven away again. The study on the 150 Attack from White's side (46 likes) says this directly:
the bishop goes to g5 or f4 and h3 comes later, so Black has only moved the same knight twice. Compare 5.f3, which takes g4 away for good. It
is not a blunder, and strong players do try it; the sources only say it gains nothing. } 6. Bg5 { [%cal Gh2h3] The most played reply
among 2400+ players. } ( 6. Bf4 { The other bishop move; h3 follows here as well. } ) )
6. f3 b5 *
